<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malietoa Laupepa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1841">1841</a>–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malietoa Laupepa was a Samoan paramount chief recognised as King of Samoa for much of the late 19th century. His rule was contested by rival chiefs such as Tupua Tamasese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mataafa-iosefo">Mataʻafa Iosefo</a>, each backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The Germans deported him in 1887, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> Treaty of 1889 restored him, and he reigned until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malietoa-laupepa.jpg" alt="Malietoa Laupepa" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Muir &amp; Moodie photography studio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laupepa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
